✅ QUESTION 4: MINIMUM COST TO CONNECT ALL ROPES

(Greedy + Min Heap)

This is a classic greedy problem and appears very often in interviews & coding rounds.

📌 Problem Statement

You are given n ropes, each with a certain length.
You need to connect all ropes into one single rope.

Rules:

Connecting two ropes of lengths a and b costs a + b

The resulting rope has length a + b

Total cost is the sum of all connections

🔹 Find the minimum total cost to connect all ropes.

💡 Key Insight (Greedy Choice)

Always connect the two smallest ropes first.

Why?

Larger ropes added later increase cost less

This is similar to Huffman Coding

🧠 Algorithm Steps

Insert all rope lengths into a Min Heap

While heap size > 1:

Remove two smallest ropes

Add their sum to total cost

Insert the combined rope back

Return total cost

⏱ Time & Space Complexity

Time: O(n log n)

Space: O(n)

☕ Java Code (Using PriorityQueue)
import java.util.*;

public class MinCostToConnectRopes {

    public static int minCost(int[] ropes) {

        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int rope : ropes)
            pq.add(rope);

        int totalCost = 0;

        while (pq.size() > 1) {

            int first = pq.poll();
            int second = pq.poll();

            int cost = first + second;
            totalCost += cost;

            pq.add(cost);
        }

        return totalCost;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] ropes = {4, 3, 2, 6};

        System.out.println(minCost(ropes));
    }
}

🧩 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 Min Heap Creation
PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();


Java’s PriorityQueue works as a min heap by default.

🔹 Insert Ropes
pq.add(rope);


Adds all rope lengths into heap.

🔹 Core Greedy Loop
while (pq.size() > 1)


Continue until one rope remains.

🔹 Pick Two Smallest
int first = pq.poll();
int second = pq.poll();


Greedy choice → smallest cost first.

🔹 Update Cost
totalCost += cost;
pq.add(cost);


Add connection cost and push combined rope back.

🔍 Example Walkthrough
Input
Ropes = [4, 3, 2, 6]

Step 1: Min Heap
[2, 3, 4, 6]

Step 2: First Merge
2 + 3 = 5
Cost = 5
Heap → [4, 5, 6]

Step 3: Second Merge
4 + 5 = 9
Total Cost = 5 + 9 = 14
Heap → [6, 9]

Step 4: Final Merge
6 + 9 = 15
Total Cost = 14 + 15 = 29

✅ Final Answer
29

